    sections: [
      { heading: "The twelve animals", paragraphs: ["The Chinese zodiac, known as shengxiao, assigns one of twelve animals to each year in a repeating cycle. The order is Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog and Pig, and it is tied to the twelve earthly branches used in the traditional Chinese calendar, from Zi for the Rat to Hai for the Pig. A popular story explains the order as the result of a race to the Jade Emperor, in which the clever Rat rode on the Ox's back and leapt ahead at the finish, while the Pig arrived last after stopping to eat. Each animal has a traditional character, from the resourceful Rat to the generous Pig, and those characters are used as a lens on personality and relationships. The animal pages describe each one's character, work style, relationships and growth edges."] },
      { heading: "Lunar New Year boundaries", paragraphs: ["Animal years begin at Lunar New Year, which falls on a different date each year between January 21 and February 20. Anyone born in January or early February therefore needs to check the date before assuming their animal. Someone born on January 20, 1990, for instance, is a Snake rather than a Horse, because Lunar New Year that year fell on January 27, while someone born on February 1, 1990 is a Horse. Unified Reading uses a table of Lunar New Year dates from 1924 to 2044 to place each birth in the correct zodiac year. Outside that range it assumes a February 5 boundary, which is close but not exact. The Chinese zodiac calculator on this site shows the Lunar New Year date for the year you enter and whether your birthday falls before it."] },
      { heading: "Fixed elements and year elements", paragraphs: ["Two kinds of element appear in the Chinese zodiac, and they are easy to confuse. Each animal has a fixed element tied to its earthly branch: the Rat and Pig are Water, the Tiger and Rabbit are Wood, the Snake and Horse are Fire, the Monkey and Rooster are Metal, and the Ox, Dragon, Goat and Dog are Earth. Separately, every year has a year element from the ten-year cycle of heavenly stems, which runs Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal and Water, two years each. Combining the two cycles produces sixty unique year combinations, such as Metal Horse or Water Rabbit, before the pattern repeats. A Unified Reading reports the year element because it distinguishes people who share an animal, while the compatibility pages use the fixed elements and the generating and controlling cycles of the five elements."] },
      { heading: "Yin and yang", paragraphs: ["Polarity is the third layer. In the twelve-animal cycle, the animals alternate between yang and yin: the Rat, Tiger, Dragon, Horse, Monkey and Dog are yang, and the Ox, Rabbit, Snake, Goat, Rooster and Pig are yin. Years follow the same rhythm, so even-numbered years in the Western calendar are yang and odd-numbered years are yin. Yang is traditionally associated with outward, active and expanding qualities, and yin with inward, receptive and consolidating ones. Neither is better; the tradition treats them as complementary halves of a whole, each necessary to the other. Polarity helps explain why animals two branches apart share a quality, and why opposite animals on the wheel, which always share polarity, can feel both familiar and opposed. The pair pages note polarity alongside the five-element relationship and the classical harmony and clash structures."] },
    ],
